/**
 * Translations for copy that lives in code rather than in messages/*.json.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS
 *
 * The homepage's checks, situation cards, service panels and area tiles are
 * authored in src/lib/homepage-content.ts as English strings, and the
 * components that render them carry a few English labels of their own. The
 * language switch changed the nav and the hero and left all of that in
 * English, which the owner saw on 10 September 2026 and asked to be fixed.
 *
 * The approach is a dictionary keyed by the exact English string. `tr()`
 * looks a string up for a locale and returns the English when there is no
 * entry, so nothing can ever go blank; `localizeDeep()` walks a content
 * object and translates every string field it finds, which is how a whole
 * panel array is localised in one call at the page boundary. English stays
 * the single source of truth and the key, so a copy edit in English is
 * visible here as a missing translation rather than a silent divergence.
 *
 * WHAT THE TRANSLATIONS ARE
 *
 * Written for the reader, not word for word. The owner's instruction was to
 * adapt the content so it fits the language and the people reading it
 * without losing its essence. Suburb names take the script of the page, as
 * the existing message files already do; MHADA, NOC, RERA, WhatsApp and BHK
 * stay in Latin because that is how people in these suburbs write them.
 * Figures are never translated: a rupee value passes through untouched.
 */

export type CopyLocale = "hi" | "mr" | "gu";

